Perfect. Since your Dispatch Agent exposes only one endpoint (POST /chat), every test goes through that endpoint. The LLM decides which MCP tool to invoke based on your prompt.

Below are the curl commands I’d use to validate the entire agent end-to-end.

⸻

1. Create a Work Order

This should call:

create_work_order
curl -N -X POST http://localhost:9106/chat \
-H "Content-Type: application/json" \
-d '{
  "message":"Create a work order for claim CLAIM-001 (claim number CLM-2026-1001). Dispatch expert EXP-101 John Smith (Field Adjuster). Schedule the visit for 2026-08-08 at 10:30 AM. Customer address is 221B Baker Street, Pune. Assigned by Vendor Manager. Estimated arrival is 10:15 AM. Estimated cost is 2500. Priority is High. Notes: Inspect structural damage. Customer phone is 9876543210. Customer email is customer@test.com."
}'

Expected MCP Tool:

create_work_order

Expected DB changes:

* ✅ work_orders → 1 new row
* ✅ dispatch_logs → 1 new row (“Created”)

⸻

2. Get Work Order

Replace WO-XXXXXX with the work_order_id created above.

Should call

get_work_order
curl -N -X POST http://localhost:9106/chat \
-H "Content-Type: application/json" \
-d '{
  "message":"Show me work order WO-XXXXXX."
}'

Expected:

* No DB changes
* Returns work order details

